+# Specialty Directory Assembled in a Read Model
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The specialty directory lists every stored specialty with the veterinarians holding it. Two of its rules cannot be answered from the veterinarian roster: a specialty no veterinarian holds is still listed, and a veterinarian holding no specialty is named nowhere. The directory therefore needs a read that starts from specialties, and the `vet` package has none — `Specialty` is reachable only as the far side of `Vet`'s owning many-to-many association.
+
+The order the page presents is also undetermined. The clinic's three vendor schemas differ in collation, and one existing case-sensitivity divergence between them is already a known defect.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Bidirectional association** — add an inverse collection of veterinarians to `Specialty` and read the directory from a specialty repository alone.
+2. **Read model over two reads** — read specialties and veterinarians independently and assemble the pairing in an immutable type the controller delegates to.
+3. **Assemble in the controller** — group the two collections inline in the request-handling method.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 2. A specialty repository and the existing veterinarian repository are each read whole, and an immutable read model pairs them. The read model imposes the presentation order in Java: specialties by name, veterinarians by last name then first name.
+
+Option 1 makes `Specialty` navigable back to `Vet`. `Vet`'s side of the association is eagerly fetched and open sessions are not held across view rendering. The inverse side must therefore be eager too — which closes a fetch cycle — or lazy, which fails when the view touches it. Option 1 also promotes a lookup value into a type that reaches an aggregate it does not own.
+
+Option 3 puts the directory's rules in a controller, which the pattern catalog's *Web controller* row does not admit. Option 3 also leaves five of the requirement's rules testable only through the HTTP surface.
+
+Ordering in Java rather than in SQL keeps the page identical on all three supported vendors, whose collations do not agree.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The directory's rules are unit-testable against real `Vet` and `Specialty` instances, with no HTTP surface and no database.
+- `Specialty` stays a leaf lookup value, matching `PetType`, and the `vet` package keeps its acyclic shape.
+- Both collections are read whole on every request. This is bounded by the clinic's roster size and matches what the veterinarian list already reads; it is not a paged read.
+- The veterinarian read is cached and the specialty read is not, so a specialty added out of band appears before a change to who holds it does. The application has no write path for either, so the window only opens under direct database edits.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts)
+- [Feature-Package Organization Without a Service Layer](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md)
+- [Script-Managed Schema Across Three Database Vendors](2026-07-31-script-managed-multi-vendor-schema.md)
